<article class="env-example" id="06T8">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T8">Example <span data-tag="06T8">5.6.1.4</span> <span class="named">(Semidirect Products)</span>.</a> Let $G$ and $N$ be groups and let $\alpha $ be an action of $G$ on $N$: that is, a group homomorphism from $G$ to the automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}(N)$. Let $BG$ and $BN$ denote the associated one-object categories (see Remark <a data-tag="00BS" href="/tag/00BS">1.3.2.4</a>), so that $\alpha $ can be identified with a strict functor $\mathscr {F}: BG \rightarrow \mathbf{Cat}$ carrying the unique object of $BG$ to the category $BN$. Then $\int _{BG} \mathscr {F}$ can be identified with the category $B\widetilde{G}$, where $\widetilde{G} = G \ltimes N$ denotes the semidirect product of $G$ and $N$. </p>
<p>More generally, for any short exact sequence of groups </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  0 \rightarrow N \rightarrow \widetilde{G} \rightarrow G \rightarrow 0, \]
</div>
<p> we can identify $B\widetilde{G}$ with the category of elements $\int _{BG} \mathscr {F}$ for some (possibly non-strict) functor of bicategories $\mathscr {F}: BG \rightarrow \mathbf{Cat}$ carrying the unique object of $BG$ to $BN$. See Corollary <a data-tag="01SU" href="/tag/01SU">5.6.5.21</a> for a more general statement. </p>
</article>
